Z||000004||Raydar||05-20-2001||12:04 PM||||As for Dennis Addlebrain... Screw 'im!<BR>But think about this...<BR>WE know how much fun our cars are to drive.  Most of us don't have any Fieros that will ever be collectible, anyway. <BR>If the Fiero ever becomes truly collectible, all the speculators will snap 'em up and put them away, never to see the light of day again. Do we really want that to happen?<BR>The only positive side to this is the possible availability of more parts, but I really think we do okay, now. (This coming from an owner of 88s.)     <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||6
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||05-20-2001||12:26 PM||||It could have easily read<P>"In the 1950's Chevy gave us the Corvette, then fortunately took it away. It was a good idea that never reached its full potential before dwindling sales killed it off." <P>It never happened. Why? Because Chevy kept plugging away at it even as sales were dwindling. It would seem that Chevy kept making improvements despite brutal competition from the T-Bird. Fieros even in 88 with no advertising probably still sold more than Vette that year.  <P>Double standard? Hypocrisy?<P>I think so.||||reg||8
